We have now defined the abstract scope, and much of the infrastructure but we have to actually add them to our web service definitions for all existing v2 (routed) web services.

Note: We’re only talking about new web services.

This is more complicated than we originally imagined.

Originally we had planned to add scopes in the route as:

{noformat}#[route(
    scopes: [
        new \core_user\route\scope\read(),
    ],
)]
{noformat}

However I realised that this will _not_ work for us because attributes will attempt to instantiate the class upon invocation via reflection by design. This is fine for Moodle core in 5.3, but where we have a plugin in the community which spans multiple versions of Moodle and which uses this syntax in a web service installed in Moodle 5.2 or earlier, the class will not exist and an exception will be thrown.

The solution I first considered here was to use a _string_ instead of a class instantiation, and then to instantiate the class at run-time.

{noformat}#[route(
    scopes: [
        \core_user\route\scope\read::class,
    ],
)]
{noformat}

I've also learned about a poorly documented part of the OpenAPI schema which allows us to have _optional_ or _alternate_ scopes which I think we do need to support.

My initial thought was to therefore support scope _sets_ and the string scopes.

This would be a case of adding a {{scopes}} to the existing {{route}} attribute and accepting an array of string arrays.

{noformat}#[route(
    scopes: [
        [
            \core_user\route\scope\read::class,
            \core_user\route\scope\write::class
        ],
        [
            \core_user\route\scope\read_any::class,
            \core_user\route\scope\write_any::class,
        ],
    ]
{noformat}

This example shows two scope _sets_, each with two scopes.

Each set of _scopes_ is ANDable The scope _sets_ are ORable.

That is:

{noformat}Either:
    \core_user\oauth2\scope\read::class AND
    \core_user\oauth2\scope\write::class

OR:
    \core_user\oauth2\scope\read_any::class AND
    \core_user\oauth2\scope\write_any::class
{noformat}

There are some additional cases here:

# No scopes specified in the route
# Scope specified but empty
# Scope set to a value which does not exist in that version of Moodle

h3. No scopes specified in the route

{noformat}#[route(
)]
{noformat}

This is equivalent to no scopes are _required_. may be for a _public_ facing web service. Ideally we should only support this for a transition period and then deprecate it.

h3. Scope specified but empty

{noformat}#[route(
    scopes: [],
)]
{noformat}

This is also equivalent to no scopes are _required_. This may be for a _public_ facing web service.

h3. Scope set to a value which does not exist in that version of Moodle

{noformat}#[route(
    scopes: [
        [
            \core_user\oauth2\scope\some_scope_that_we_will_create_in_the_future::class,
        ],
    ],
)]
{noformat}

In this case we must not error, but we must instead throw away the *entire scope set*. If we were to _ignore_ the scope absence, then we would essentially be converting it to a scope which has no access restrictions.

In the case where scopes _were_ specified, but did not have an impact, we must use a value which can never be fulfilled instead.

After further thought I realised that this is largely on the right lines, but instead of an attribute on the route attribute, this should really be an attribute of its own and that we should avoid overloading the {{route}} attribute.

Enter…

h2. The scope _attribute_

The alternate to this is to use a top-level attribute:

{code:javascript}class my_controller {
    #[\core\router\scopeset(
        new \core_user\route\scope\user\read(),
        new \core_user\route\scope\user\write(),
    )]
    #[\core\router\scopeset(
        new \core_user\route\scope\user\read_any(),
        new \core_user\route\scope\user\write_any(),
    )]
    public function serve() {
        // ...
    }
}
{code}

The benefit here is that we can still use the {{new}} incantation, and that a missing scope will lead to an Error which can handle more logically:

{noformat}$rcm = new \ReflectionMethod(my_controller::class, 'serve');
$attributes = $rcm->getAttributes(\core\oauth2\scope\set::class);
$hasanyscopes = count($attributes) > 0;
$scopesets = [];

foreach ($attributes as $attribute) {
    try {
        $scopesets[] = $attribute->newInstance();
    } catch (\Error $e) {
        // The scope class specified in this set does not exist - discard the entire set.
    }
}

if ($hasanyscopes && empty($scopesets)) {
    // This is the fallback case.
}
{noformat}

This approach also allows us to specify a {{\core\router\scopes\unfulfilled_scope}} attribute to be _explicit_ about not requiring scopes.

h2. Conclusion

I’ve opted to go for the new {{scopeset}} attribute as this avoids overloading the route attribute and gives greater flexibility. this is the approach we should take for any new related property in the future.

*Reproduction*

# On an _uninstalled_ Moodle 5.2 checkout run {{composer install}} from the Moodle root
# In the same checkout, switch to the latest main branch, retaining the existing *vendor* directory and do *not* run Composer again
# Install the site (either via CLI or web)

The installer emits repeated warnings:

{noformat}Cannot retrieve value of undefined directive URI.AllowedSymbols{noformat}

Installation still reports “Installation completed successfully”. There is no clear explanation that the installed Composer dependencies do not match the Moodle codebase.

*Additional weirdness*

# On an _installed_ Moodle 5.2 site run {{composer install}}
# In the same checkout, switch to latest main, retaining the existing *vendor* directory and do *not* run composer again
# Upgrade the site (note that there is no indication that anything is wrong)
# Run the following script:
{code:php}<?php
define('CLI_SCRIPT', true);
require_once('config.php');

$html = '<a href="<https://example.invalid/course/view.php?id=123&amp;section=2>">Section</a>';
echo clean_text($html, FORMAT_HTML), PHP_EOL;{code}

The upgrade completes successfully, but HTML cleaning emits the same warning and changes the link to:

{code:html}<a href="https://example.invalid/course/view.php?id%3D123%26section%3D2">Section</a>{code}

In this example, the retained Composer installation provides HTML Purifier 4.18.0, while main requires 4.19.0. Moodle ends up using bundled HTML Purifier code with configuration schema data from the older Composer package.

*Suggested fix*

Detect the mismatch at install/upgrade time and advise to either remove the vendor directory or run {{composer update}}
